  "scriptId": "age-03-sums-differences-linear-equations",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Age Sums, Differences, and Linear Equations",
    "description": "Combine age totals and age gaps to solve for unknown individual ages, then check each result against the stated relationship.",
    "teachingApproach": "Use short, connected phone-sized teaching screens with concrete ages, labelled dates, separate calculation lines, a reason for each step, and an original-clue check. Place a fresh matching practice question after each worked example; wrong answers receive a worked correction and parallel practice.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 8, Problems on Ages. Approved scope: Model sums and differences of two ages with x+y=S and x−y=D, derive the half-sum/half-difference method, and use a ratio or a simple linear relation when paired with a total or gap. Explain why adding and subtracting the equations isolates the unknowns. Include relationships at a specified time, a sum or difference that changes when the date changes, and a direct substitution check. Learners should distinguish a stated sum from a sum of ages several years later, track the number of people affected by a time shift, preserve which age is larger, and recognize when the available equation set is insufficient. Keep equations at the chapter's elementary linear level."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's find ages from clues about totals and gaps.\n\nIf two people are 12 years apart, that tells us something. If their ages also total 54, we can find both ages. Let's see how the clues work together.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Imagine two ages that add to 50. The older person is 14 years older.\n\nFirst remove those extra 14 years from the total:\n50 − 14 = 36.\n\nNow the two ages would be equal.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Split 36 equally: 36 ÷ 2 = 18.\n\nYounger: 18\nOlder: 18 + 14 = 32\n\nCheck both clues: 18 + 32 = 50 and 32 − 18 = 14.\n\nWhy does this work? If older = x and younger = y, then x + y = 50 and x − y = 14. Subtract the second clue from the first: 2y = 36. Use that remove-and-split idea next.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
      "type": "CHOICE",
      "content": {
        "text": "Two people’s present ages sum to 54 years and differ by 12 years. Find the older age.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "input": {
        "type": "CHOICE",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "a",
            "label": "21"
          },
          {
            "id": "b",
            "label": "33"
          },
          {
            "id": "c",
            "label": "27"
          },
          {
            "id": "d",
            "label": "36"
          }
        ]
      },
      "questionReference": {
