    "teach_4": {
      "id": "teach_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The same total-and-gap idea works for a parent and child. Their ages add to 60, and the parent is 24 years older.\n\nTake the extra 24 out of the total. The remaining 36 is two copies of the child's age.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_4_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4_step": {
      "id": "teach_4_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Child = (60 − 24) ÷ 2 = 18.\nParent = 18 + 24 = 42.\n\nCheck: 18 + 42 = 60; 42 − 18 = 24. A family relationship does not change the arithmetic; it helps us name the older person.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_4"
        }
      ]
    },
